---
import LegalLayout from '../../layouts/LegalLayout.astro';
import { OFERTAS_HIPOTECAS, FECHA_DATOS_ISO, fechaOferta } from '../../constants/hipotecas-bancos';
import { currentEuribor } from '../../constants/euribor-values';

const numBancos = new Set(OFERTAS_HIPOTECAS.map((o) => o.banco)).size;
const numOfertas = OFERTAS_HIPOTECAS.length;
---
<LegalLayout
  title="Methodology and editorial policy | Hipotecalc"
  description={`Where Hipotecalc's data comes from: the ${numBancos}-lender mortgage comparison, the official ECB Euribor and the ITP guides, with sources and review frequency.`}
  heading="Methodology and editorial policy"
  path="/en/methodology"
  lang="en"
  lastUpdated="September 2026"
>
  <p>
    Hipotecalc is an informational tool. Its usefulness depends on you being able
    to trust the numbers, so this page explains where every figure comes from,
    how often it is reviewed, and what we do when something changes or we get it
    wrong.
  </p>


  <h2>1. Who produces and who signs the content</h2>
  <p>
    The tools and data are built and maintained by the
    <a href="/en/about">Hipotecalc team</a> — two people: a developer and a
    finance expert with a long fintech career. The editorial financial content
    (guides, blog and regional ITP guides) is authored by
    <a href="/en/authors/jose-perales">José Perales</a>, mortgage advisor and
    real estate credit intermediary registered with the Bank of Spain under
    number E286. Every page shows, under its title, who signs it and when it
    was last updated.
  </p>

  <h2>2. The mortgage comparison</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href="/en/mortgage-comparison">comparison</a> currently covers
    {numOfertas} offers from {numBancos} lenders and brands.
    Current data comes from <strong>official lender websites</strong> and their
    pre-contractual documents or calculators. We only include products with
    verifiable TIN and APR. Each offer’s details show the periods, discounts,
    fees and requirements we could check. We retain sources in our editorial
    records so each figure can be checked again.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Review frequency:</strong> the comparison is reviewed
      <strong> at least once a month</strong>, alongside the publication of the
      monthly Euribor average, and whenever we detect a relevant change at an
      institution. The date of the latest extraction is shown in the table
      header (currently: {fechaOferta(FECHA_DATOS_ISO, 'en')}).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Rate evolution:</strong> arrows are shown only when the previous
      and current values share the same product, term, discounts and source.
      A different example or source does not establish a rate increase or decrease.
      This review replaces earlier estimates with official sources and does not
      present those replacements as trends.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>What we don't do:</strong> no bank pays to appear, for its
      position or for its figures. We include institutions based on their
      relevance in the Spanish market and the availability of verifiable prices,
      whether or not they have any commercial relationship with us.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Limits of the data:</strong> published rates are each bank's
      commercial starting point; your personalised offer (FEIN) may differ based
      on your profile. We show discounted and standard rates when both have been verified. Compare APRs for equivalent amounts, terms and products. Variable APR is not a forecast of future costs, and the Euribor shown in the header does not replace the lender’s assumptions.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>3. The Euribor</h2>
  <p>
    The 12-month Euribor values come from the
    <a href="https://data.ecb.europa.eu/data/datasets/FM/FM.M.U2.EUR.RT.MM.EURIBOR1YD_.HSTA" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">official European Central Bank series</a>,
    which an automated process downloads and publishes on the site every month,
    when the ECB releases the monthly average (latest:
    {currentEuribor.value.toLocaleString('en-US')}%, {currentEuribor.labelEn}).
    The index is administered by
    <a href="https://www.emmi-benchmarks.eu/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">EMMI</a>,
    its original source, and can be cross-checked at the
    <a href="https://www.bde.es/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Bank of Spain</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>4. The ITP and tax guides</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href="/en/itp">regional ITP guides</a> are written from the
    consolidated legal texts in the BOE, the regional and foral gazettes and the
    Spanish Ministry of Finance's annual "Tributación Autonómica" inventory.
    Each guide cites the exact legislation behind every rate and relief, links
    to the official sources and shows its verification date. They are reviewed
    whenever the legislation changes and at least once per tax year.
  </p>

  <h2>5. The calculators</h2>
  <p>
    All calculators use the standard <strong>French amortization</strong>
    formula — the one Spanish banks apply — and calculations run in your
    browser: the data you enter is never sent to any server. Results are
    indicative estimates; the assumptions and limits are detailed in the
    <a href="/en/financial-disclaimer">financial disclaimer</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>6. Corrections</h2>
  <p>
    If you spot a wrong or outdated figure — a rate a bank no longer offers, a
    regional relief that has changed — write to us through
    the site's contact channels. We verify against the
    official source within a reasonable time and, where appropriate, correct
    it, record the change and update the review date shown under the title of
    the affected page.
  </p>

  <h2>7. Editorial independence</h2>
  <p>
    Hipotecalc has a <strong>commercial lead-referral agreement</strong> with
    <a href="/en/authors/jose-perales">José Perales</a>, the mortgage advisor
    behind the site's contact buttons: if you end up working with him,
    Hipotecalc may receive compensation for the introduction. We also explain
    this in <a href="/en/about">about us</a>. That agreement plays no part in
    the data: the comparison, the guides and the calculators are produced under
    the criteria on this page, and no financial institution reviews or approves
    our content before publication.
  </p>
</LegalLayout>
